S cbn “AT 2:20 THE TITANIC FOUNDERED.”
P Shortly afterward the following was given out as the message
  from Captain Haddock:
P “At 2:20 a. m. the Titanic foundered. Carpathia proceeding to New
  York with passengers. H. J. HADDOCK, S. S. Olympic.”
P After this message was handed to Vice-President Franklin he said:
P “It is horrible!
P “As far as we know, it has been rumored from Halifax that three
  steamers have passengers on board, namely, the Virginian, the Car-
  pathia and the Parisian.
P “Now we have heard from Captain Haddock that the Titanic sank
  at 2:20 this morning. We have also learned from him that the Car-
  pathia had 675 survivors on board.
P “It is very difficult to learn if the Virginian and the Parisian have
  any survivors on board. We have asked Captain Haddock and our
  agent at Halifax to ascertain if there are any passengers aboard the
  two steamships.
P “We very much fear, however, that there has been a great loss of
  life, but it is impossible for us to give further particulars until we have
  heard from the Virginian and the Parisian. We have no information
  that there are any passengers aboard those two vessels.
P “We are very hopeful that the rumors reaching us that the Vir-
  ginian and the Parisian have survivors aboard are true.
P “There was a sufficient number of lifeboats on the Titanic to carry
  all the passengers.
P “I was confident to-day, when I made the statement that the Titanic
  was unsinkable, that the steamship was safe and that there would be
  no loss of life. The first definite news came in the message from Cap-
  tain Haddock.”
P Mr. Franklin denied emphatically that the White Star Line had
  withheld the report of the sinking of the Titanic. He said that the
  wireless message from Captain Haddock was made public by the com-
  pany a few minutes after it was received in the offices.
P White Star officials said last night that the Titanic carried 1,323
  passengers, of whom 318 were first class, 262 second class and 743
  steerage.
P The crew numbered 890, including officers, men and stewardesses.
P After the message was received from Captain Haddock the White
  Star Line cancelled its orders for special trains to proceed to Halifax
  to bring to New York the rescued passengers of the Titanic.
P The disaster is the greatest known to shipping history.
P Those nearest approaching it were the loss of the steamer Atlan-
  tic, in 1873, with 574 lives, and the sinking of La Bourgogne, in 1898,
  with 571 lives.
P There still remains the hope that the Virginian and the Parisian
  will report having saved some of the passengers or crew of the Titanic.
P It was not until after 7 o’clock last night that it became known that
  the Titanic had foundered with a terrible loss of life. All day hopes
  had been buoyed up by messages telling of the rescue of all on board,
  and it was also reported that the vessel would be towed safely into
  harbor at Halifax.
S cbn THE EARLY MESSAGES HOPEFUL, BUT UNOFFICIAL.
P The early messages, however, were all unofficial, and there was
  always the fear of the bad news that came flashing over the wires from
  Cape Race soon after 7 o’clock that the Titanic had sunk south of the
  Newfoundland Banks three hours and fifty-five minutes after striking
  an iceberg.
P The first official information, sent by Captain Haddock of the
  Titanic’s sister ship, the Olympic, told the awful extent of the disaster
  —that of the more than 2,200 persons on the Titanic only 675 had been
  saved. The significant line was added that nearly all of those saved
  were women and children.
P There were 230 women and children in the first and second cabins.
  It is not known how many there were in the steerage, but the dispatch
  seems to spell the doom of nearly the entire crew as well as the men
  passengers.
S cbn HELP FROM VIRGINIAN AND PARISIAN NOT PROBABLE.
P In the confusion reigning at the White Star offices Vice-President
  Franklin studied the situation as calmly as possible. In spite of hopes
  to the contrary, he did not believe the Virginian and the Parisian could
  have reached the scene of the disaster in time to be of assistance.
P When the Virginian first reported her receipt of the “S O S”
  signal on Sunday night she said she probably would not be able to
  reach the Titanic before 10 a. m. yesterday. This would have been
  nearly eight hours after the Titanic sank. It was equally doubtful if
  the Parisian could have reached the doomed vessel earlier.
P Mr. Franklin said that from his knowledge of Captain Smith’s
  gallantry and heroism on other occasions he believed the veteran officer
  stuck to the bridge and sank with his vessel.
